WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:03.590
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:07.950
Pixar's latest offering, Finding Dory, sees
the animated hit-makers dip their toes back

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:12.099
into the sea of sequels after treating us
to two original features last year.

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:17.050
A follow-up to 2003's Finding Nemo, Finding
Dory sees the forgetful blue tang move from

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:22.869
side-kick to centre-stage in a fishy tale
which focuses on her search for her long-lost

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:23.869
parents.

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:27.630
Screenwriters Victoria Strouse and Andrew
Stanton, who also directs alongside Angus

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.830
MacLane, have rounded out Dory's character,
highlighting not just her sweet and funny

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:36.989
side, but also lending her more depth, revealing
the fears and worries she faces due to her

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:38.700
short-term memory loss.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.800
And as the voice of Dory, Ellen DeGeneres
is fantastic at bringing all those different

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.680
shades of her character to life.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.210
This time we also get a young version of Dory
too.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:53.160
The way she's animated in flashbacks is oh-so
adorably cute, and Sloane Murray and Lucia

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.770
Geddes do a great job lending their voices
to baby and tween versions of the absent-minded

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:58.770
fish.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.970
As well as bringing back several Finding Nemo
fixtures including Nemo himself and his dad

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:07.880
Marlin, Finding Dory introduces us to a whole
range of wonderful new characters.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:13.090
Of the newcomers, a grumpy but ingenious seven-tentacled
octopus called Hank, who's brilliantly voiced

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:16.790
by Modern Family star Ed O'Neill, gets the
lion's share of screentime.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:21.670
And what a fantastic addition he is to this
underwater world, making a great odd couple

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.400
with Dory, providing tons of laughs and extra
heart too.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.579
As the near-sighted whale shark Destiny and
the rather melodramatic beluga whale Bailey,

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:34.310
Kaitlin Olson and Modern Family's Ty Burrell
add loads of lovely moments, playing really

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.820
well off each other and also Ellen's Dory.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.890
And Idris Elba and Dominic West's sunbathing
sea lions are an absolute hoot!

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.259
There are also some pretty cool cameos in
the film, with one in particular used in a

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:48.990
clever way that's guaranteed to bring a big
smile to your face whenever you hear it.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:53.500
As you'd expect from Pixar, the animation
is top-notch, with an array of gorgeous scenes

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:58.990
set in familiar and new ocean locations from
the coral reef to kelp forests, and lots of

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.710
interesting land-bound locales provided by
a rehabilitation centre for aquatic life on

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:03.710
the California coast.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:07.780
There is a feeling about Finding Dory that
we're retreading some of the same water we

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:13.140
covered in Finding Nemo, both thematically
and in terms of some of the ocean-set scenes

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:17.230
in particular, and it's not as nuanced as
Pixar's best, but there's still lots of fun

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:21.630
to be had with some great characters, humorous
moments, and action-filled sequences that

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:26.340
make the movie worth watching, as well as
an underlying message about disability that

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.090
deserves to be heard.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:32.490
And while Pixar's last feature, The Good Dinosaur,
had more grown-up humour than I first expected

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:38.180
in a movie that was more squarely pitched
at kids than say, Inside Out; Finding Dory

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.800
doesn't quite hit the creatively clever heights
that we've come to expect of Pixar when it

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.220
comes to adult laughs, though you'll still
find a decent number of them.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.570
By the way, if you're an easter egg hunter,
don't forget to keep your eyes peeled throughout,

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.270
and I'll have my video guide to the movie's
easter eggs right here when it's ready.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:58.840
Oh, and if you usually dash out of the cinema
as soon as the film proper's finished, just

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.990
be aware that there are some lovely animated
moments during the credits and at the very

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:07.170
end of the credits there's a little treat
too, so make sure you don't miss that!

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:13.230
So, to sum up: Finding Dory isn't quite unforgettable,
but it is beautifully animated and full of

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:18.170
warmth, and features a nice mix of colourful
characters and a voice cast who really deliver

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:19.170
the goods.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.930
And while it's not a 100% fresh catch of the
day, overall, it's plenty enjoyable and entertaining.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:28.730
And on top of that, the short film, Piper,
which screens before Finding Dory is a lovely

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:29.730
little delight.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.200
Finding Dory gets 4 out of 5 Flicks.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.300
Now, what did you think of Finding Dory?

